{{!
    @template theme_dimensions/category_directory

    The root of the course index, drawn as a directory of categories. A course
    never sits directly in the top-level pseudo-category, so the page has no
    course grid and no pager: it is the search line, then one poster per
    top-level category the viewer may discover, drawn by the areas partial a
    category page organised by area uses, with the panel of the open one between
    the rows. That panel lists the area's whole subtree, since a panel of a
    top-level category's direct courses would always be empty. Every category
    page below the root keeps the recursive listing.

    The root counts nothing at rest, so no poster can be known to be empty: every
    poster opens a panel, and an empty one says so inside it. No poster wears a
    number at rest either - how much an area holds is a fact about a listing the
    reader has not opened, and the panel says it once open. Without JavaScript a
    poster is a link reloading this page with its panel open; for a real account
    the panel module turns it into a button and fetches the panel.

    Past population::DIRECTORY_SLICE posters the rest sit inside a closed native
    disclosure, drawn open when the open poster is among them. It is a page cut
    and not a data cut - every poster is built by the same server pass - and,
    because every image and crest loads lazily, a browser fetches the hidden
    posters' pictures only once they are revealed.

    The empty state talks about categories and not about courses: a reader who
    may browse no top-level category has not been told that the site holds no
    course.

    The root reads the search and no other criterion. The search line comes
    first (category_searchline); under a term every poster wears how many matches
    its area holds, a poster with none leaves the grid for the summary of areas
    with no result, and the areas' own box says when nothing matched anywhere.
    For a real account the listing's module runs here, reading the vitrine
    payload, and searches as the reader types; without it the line's form
    submits.

    The container names itself as the showcase's region like the listing does,
    which keeps one body class and one stylesheet over both pages.

